vironment with so much science so much  excellent science going on and the  development of  of new techniques cutting-edge  techniques you really get ideas and with  the people that that are here who are  sharing this special summer time and and  those relationships persist and that  really has a powerful impact on on my  research and the research of many others  the level of interaction is way higher  than it is in the research labs so it's  nonstop fully saturating human  scientific interaction and it's like the  probability of the higher the  interaction level the higher the  probability that you'll get more than  one inspiration like coming to teach  because I remember for this short period  each year what fun it is to do research  I get to block everything out and just  think about science I think that the  courses are a great opportunity to  basically interact with students you  basically get input from students and a  lot of times you know their ideas are  they spark new ideas with you and it's a  great opportunity to just get more  feedback i I know how cool it is to take  the course from firsthand experience and  it really really I do strongly believe  that he changed the way I do science I  came here to learn more about biology  and ended up falling in love with cell  biology and in particular the  cytoskeleton so I came to learn a little  bit and I left having completely changed  my life if my life was a mathematical  function the two some two months that I  spent in the physiology course in 1993  was a was a discontinuity or a  singularity that left the shape of my  life being completely different after  the course I've been coming down to the  MBL for three summers as a co-director  of a course and that's been an amazing  experience so the thing I really love  about these courses is that we have a  mixture of students postdocs and faculty  who come from around the world and they  spent three weeks here where they  interact with each other and learn a lot  from the faculty who passed through from  a course but learn a ton from each other  and we learned ton from them too it's  not just about you know the everyday  interactions or we know with the speaker  isn't about you know talking to other  faculty talking to people about the  research is also the fact  you know you get to teach in an  environment where people really think  hard about what you're doing and they  force you to a thing even harder so I i  guess it's also a personal belief that  through teaching one also it gets to  think harder about the problems everyone  is thinking about and try to make  connections that you maybe didn't make  originally I always learn something new  every year I always learn things I never  imagined that I would learn I especially  love teaching because I've learned a lot  from the students I learned more from  the students than from anyone else just  from hearing the research that they're  doing back home and interacting with  them on a day-to-day basis that when  we're trying to do experiments together  they always surprise me with their  ingenuity and I come away with  completely energized and it with a  totally new perspective on on my work I  mean I like coming here because I don't  think I've ever come here and not gotten  a new idea I you know there's so much  going on here I bounced off 10 million  people and I bounced a million ideas off  of people I don't get anything done in a  concrete sense but what I do get is  inspiration and new ideas and that's I  mean that's worth more than you know 100  uninspired experiments for the rest of  the year but the reasons i come here are  partly to do my own experiments with my  own hands which once you become a p.i of  a lab group becomes harder and harder to  get out of the office and at the bench  and here I sit behind a microscope and I  look at real cells growing I do  experiments which gives me just the  thrill for for why I fell in love with  science and I get to rediscover that all  over again myself and just to come down  and almost be a postdoc again to avoid  sitting on seminars and hear what other  people are doing and and i started a new  collaboration before lunch my first day  here so i mean just having those sort of  informal interactions with the new group  of scientists is really energizing and  really recharging and and it's been  awesome i'm hoping i can do this you  know every summer this is impossible  it's been really fantastic you have a  critical mass of people that you would  never have access to anywhere  else and also everyone is very very open  here I mean it's a very open sharing  community in terms of the ideas and the  people you can go to anybody's lab and  ask the questions you see seminars on  things you wouldn't normally go to visit  right here and so it you get many more  ideas so what I would say about coming  here that's so amazing just I can  illustrate with a story I got off the  bus three days ago and between the time  I got off the bus and I got to my house  I ran into three people and I already  had two meetings planned including a  dinner meeting and not somebody I hadn't  met before and we talked in really  amazing science so there's just so many  people here that are off-scale I really  recharge my battery scientifically  chance encounters with someone on the  sidewalk have completely changed  sometimes the course of my experiments  and that sort of only happens at mvl  where we get this critical mass of great  people wandering around excited to talk  about what problems they're interested  in and so it's an addiction but I think  it's a healthy addiction the MBL is some  sort of magical magnet that draws the  best and some of the best and most  interesting scientists from around the  world and it's so in some sense the  crossroads but is really a destination  it's a place where lots of people come  together from different places and just  for the summer months assemble labs and  interact as colleagues I like coming  here because it's a way to kind of just  get away from the everyday things that  are pulling on you and try new things  and there are people here to try new  things with and there's equipment here  to try new things with so it's a way to  explore wild ideas kind of spend a  couple of weeks and see if you can get  them to work and you would never make  the time to do that in your everyday  life at your everyday institution  another thing is that this is a place to  play and what I mean by that is that  science really is about curiosity and  investigating things because you find  them interesting and our lives at home  are often colored by a completely  different considerations so every summer  we come here and we try stuff it's an  invigorating exciting place it's got all  kinds of great people great equipment  and I'm reminded of a quote from  Sherlock Holmes where he said that when  you rule out the impossible whatever  remains no matter how improbable must be  the truth and so you come to and be able  to try the impossible and it's a short  time sometimes that you the impossible  works and that's what's cool about this  place is it you can try it and you can  go away and carry on with other things  or you get new ideas and it's just it's  just the place to try and have fun but  here creative students hard-working  loads of equipment and lots of reagents  mean that you can try lots of things  iterate and eventually find the solution  to the problem there's something really  wonderful about an atmosphere where you  try to do things just because you're  curious about them just because you've  been wanting to try something out you're  not worried about whether or not it'll  quote-unquote work or won't work  necessarily you just want to see what  will happen but you can you can easily  get out of your comfort zone and and you  know you can think of the crazies  experiment and if if more less you can  put it together people will help you do  it eh